<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.5.5: Writing Equations to Represent Exponential Decay</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01315</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.5.5: Writing Equations to Represent Exponential Decay</md:title>
    <md:uuid>022c9859-f597-41a9-9ec3-e449da46b41c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Acetaminophen is a common pain reliever and fever reducer. Here is a graph showing the amount of acetaminophen, in milligrams, in an adult’s body at different times after they take a normal dose.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of an exponential function, origin O. time (hours) and acetaminophen (mg)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6067169e5114ec7c6decefb23c92843d3d582b07"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item> What is the vertical intercept?
        <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1,000
      </item>
      
      <item> What does it mean in this situation? 
        <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> There are 1,000 mg of acetaminophen in the person’s body after taking the medicine (this is the dose size).
      </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  
  <item> What fraction of the medicine remained after one hour? 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac34\) of the medicine remained after 1 hour since \(\frac{750}{1000}=\frac{3}{4}\).
  </item>
  
  <item> Write an equation that represents the amount of medicine, \(a\), after \(t\) hours. 
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a=1,000(\frac{3}{4})^t\)
  </item>
</list>

<title> Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title> Points to emphasize</title>
<para>If students are still struggling with writing equations to represent exponential growth or decay, highlight a few examples from cool-downs and use practice problems from this lesson to provide further opportunities to examine the meaning of \(a\) and \(b\) terms and to practice writing equations to represent exponential decay.</para></section>
</content>
